    "rawSources": [
      "##### Switch Ranks (2 Acumen)\nYou command your allies to regroup, switching tired fighters for fresh ones. Choose a number of allied Creatures within 60 feet of you, up to your Intelligence Modifier or Wisdom Modifier. Each of those Creatures can swap places with a willing Creature that has a Speed of more than 0 within 10 feet of themselves, without either Creature provoking Opportunity Attacks. A Creature cannot swap into a space it does not fit in.",
      "#### 1st Level: Tactical Knowledge\nYour training as a Tactician allows you to direct the battlefield in ways others cannot; you gain access to Basic Tactics (detailed below) that are fueled by your Acumen.\n\n**Acumen:** Acumen is the resource that represents your capacity to bear the mental strain of commanding. Your maximum Acumen is equal to twice your Proficiency Bonus. You regain all expended Acumen when you complete a Long Rest.\n\n**Using Tactics:** You can activate a Tactic either as an Action or a Bonus Action by expending the required Acumen for that Tactic; this means that you can activate two Tactics in the same Turn if you have the Acumen. However, once you have used a Tactic, you cannot use that Tactic again until the start of your next Turn. \n\n**Recieving the benefit of Tactics.** Many Tactics have no defined range of effect. However, a Creature must be able to hear or see the Tactician clearly to be a valid beneficiary of that Tactic; if an allied Creature is ruled by the DM as being unable to see or hear you clearly, they are not affected by your Tactics even if they are within its affected area. This means that communication through a radio, or through some other remote means, is sufficient for many Tactics to be effective. However, waving from the horizon is not likely to be clear enough to communicate your Tactics.\n\n{{note\n##### Example: Delivering Tactics\n- The Tactician, Player A, is communicating with Player B, a Guard, through the use of Messenger Kits. Because Player B can hear Player A's instructions clearly, they can benefit when Player A uses the \"Fall Back\" Tactic on them.\n\n- Player A wants to issue the \"Volley Fire\" Tactic to Players C and D, Marksmen on a nearby roof. Players C and D do not have Messenger Kits, nor can they hear Player A, but they are close enough to easily visually make out Player A's signalling with their arm, and can this benefit from that Tactic.\n\n- Player A wants to issue the \"Move Up\" Tactic to Player E, a Caster who is 300 feet away. Player E is too far away to hear Player A's voice, does not have any other way to hear Player A, and while they can technically see them, they are so far away that they cannot make out any individual gesture. They cannot benefit from this Tactic.\n\n}}\n\nFrom 4th Level, when a Creature expends a Reaction in response to one of your Tactics while they are within your Strategic Zone, you may immediately use your Tip of the Spear Feature on that Creature regardless of range (no Action required).\n\\page\n{{pageNumber,auto}}",
      "{{note\n##### Tactics for Casters\nIn the world of Terra, Casters are an important part of battle tactica, no more or less than other warriors. For the Tactician's Tactics, this is represented in the following way: when a Tactic would allow a Creature to make a Melee or Ranged Weapon Attack as part of that Tactic, that Creature can instead cast a Cantrip that involves making a Melee or Ranged Spell Attack, respectively, to replace that Melee or Ranged Attack. However, if they do so, the exertion leaves them wobbling; they are Dazed until the end of their next Turn.\n}}"
    ],
    "classOption": {
      "category": "tactic",
      "tier": "Basic",
      "cost": "2 Acumen",
      "effect": "##### Switch Ranks (2 Acumen)\nYou command your allies to regroup, switching tired fighters for fresh ones. Choose a number of allied Creatures within 60 feet of you, up to your Intelligence Modifier or Wisdom Modifier. Each of those Creatures can swap places with a willing Creature that has a Speed of more than 0 within 10 feet of themselves, without either Creature provoking Opportunity Attacks. A Creature cannot swap into a space it does not fit in."
    },
    "ruleContext": "#### 1st Level: Tactical Knowledge\nYour training as a Tactician allows you to direct the battlefield in ways others cannot; you gain access to Basic Tactics (detailed below) that are fueled by your Acumen.\n\n**Acumen:** Acumen is the resource that represents your capacity to bear the mental strain of commanding. Your maximum Acumen is equal to twice your Proficiency Bonus. You regain all expended Acumen when you complete a Long Rest.\n\n**Using Tactics:** You can activate a Tactic either as an Action or a Bonus Action by expending the required Acumen for that Tactic; this means that you can activate two Tactics in the same Turn if you have the Acumen. However, once you have used a Tactic, you cannot use that Tactic again until the start of your next Turn. \n\n**Recieving the benefit of Tactics.** Many Tactics have no defined range of effect. However, a Creature must be able to hear or see the Tactician clearly to be a valid beneficiary of that Tactic; if an allied Creature is ruled by the DM as being unable to see or hear you clearly, they are not affected by your Tactics even if they are within its affected area. This means that communication through a radio, or through some other remote means, is sufficient for many Tactics to be effective. However, waving from the horizon is not likely to be clear enough to communicate your Tactics.\n\n{{note\n##### Example: Delivering Tactics\n- The Tactician, Player A, is communicating with Player B, a Guard, through the use of Messenger Kits. Because Player B can hear Player A's instructions clearly, they can benefit when Player A uses the \"Fall Back\" Tactic on them.\n\n- Player A wants to issue the \"Volley Fire\" Tactic to Players C and D, Marksmen on a nearby roof. Players C and D do not have Messenger Kits, nor can they hear Player A, but they are close enough to easily visually make out Player A's signalling with their arm, and can this benefit from that Tactic.\n\n- Player A wants to issue the \"Move Up\" Tactic to Player E, a Caster who is 300 feet away. Player E is too far away to hear Player A's voice, does not have any other way to hear Player A, and while they can technically see them, they are so far away that they cannot make out any individual gesture. They cannot benefit from this Tactic.\n\n}}\n\nFrom 4th Level, when a Creature expends a Reaction in response to one of your Tactics while they are within your Strategic Zone, you may immediately use your Tip of the Spear Feature on that Creature regardless of range (no Action required).\n\\page\n{{pageNumber,auto}}\n\n{{note\n##### Tactics for Casters\nIn the world of Terra, Casters are an important part of battle tactica, no more or less than other warriors. For the Tactician's Tactics, this is represented in the following way: when a Tactic would allow a Creature to make a Melee or Ranged Weapon Attack as part of that Tactic, that Creature can instead cast a Cantrip that involves making a Melee or Ranged Spell Attack, respectively, to replace that Melee or Ranged Attack. However, if they do so, the exertion leaves them wobbling; they are Dazed until the end of their next Turn.\n}}"
