      "#### 1st Level: The Essence of Ritual\nThe essence of Ritual is pre-preparation. You gain the following benefits:\n\n**Ritual Performance.** You have access to a number of Rituals. These Rituals are divided into two types; Ephemeral Rituals and Permanent Rituals.\n- **Ephemeral Rituals.** When you activate an Ephemeral Ritual, you gain that benefit until you next complete a Long Rest, after which you must activate the Ritual again to regain the benefit. \n- **Permanent Rituals.** When you activate a Permanent Ritual, you gain that benefit *permanently*, unless stated otherwise.\n\n{{align-text:left\nAll of your Ephemeral and Permanent Rituals can be found in their respective tables below.\nYou access these Rituals by meeting certain criteria, listed below; once you have met a Criteria, you cannot meet that Criteria again until you complete a Short or Long Rest. When you meet one of these Criteria, you earn 1 Essence, and can immediately activate 1 Ephemeral Ritual of your choice (no Action required). To activate a Permanent Ritual of your choice, you must expend the required amount of Essence for that Ritual by completing a 10 minute Ritual, as if you were casting a Ritual Art.\n}}\n::\n{{note\n##### Ritual Criteria\nYou earn 1 Essence when you meet one of the following Criteria; you can only meet each Criteria once per Short or Long Rest:\n\n- An Elite or Leader Creature you have damaged since the beginning of your last Turn is reduced to 0 Hit Points.\n- You score a Critical Hit.\n- You deal 30 or more Damage in a single instance (including spread across mutiple Creatures)\n- You succeed on a Skill Check by 5 or more.\n- You cast a levelled Art on an allied Creature that does not restore Hit Points or deal Damage.\n- A Creature succeeds on a roll as a result of your Aid Die.\n\n}}\n\n{{note\n##### Ritualist starting at later levels\nIf you are starting at a higher level, the Ritualist is lessened by its reliance on its Rituals and the time it takes to complete them. Thus, if you are starting at 7th Level or above, it is recommended that you choose two Permanent Rituals for you to have already completed. If you are starting at 14th Level or above, it is recommended that you instead choose four Permanent Rituals to have already completed.\n}}\n\n\n\\column\n\n**Accoutrements of Ritual.** You can conduct a 10 minute long Ritual to convert one of the following items into a Ritual Tool: A Melee Weapon that deals Piercing or Slashing Damage, an Instrument you are Proficient with, a set of Clothing, an Arts Wand or Arts Staff, or a trinket such as a ring or pendant. The item functions as a normal item of its type, but gains special benefits when equipped by you, detailed below. You can only have one Ritual Tool at a time; if you convert a new Ritual Tool when you already have your maximum amount, the oldest Ritual Tool returns to being an ordinary item. \n\n- **Arts Wand/Arts Staff.** You learn the Eldritch Blast Art; it does not count against your Arts Known, but you must be wielding this Ritual Equipment to cast it. Additionally, once per Turn when you hit a hostile Creature with the Eldritch Blast Cantrip, you can choose a willing Creature you can see within 30 feet of you (no Action required), then expend and roll one of your Aid Dice; that Creature gains Temporary Hit Points equal the amount rolled. If the Attack that triggered this effect was a Critical Hit, your Aid Die is not expended.\n- **Clothing.** While you are wearing this Clothing, and are not wearing any Armour, your base Armour Class is equal to 13 + your Spellcasting Ability Modifier. Additionally, at the beginning of your Turn, if you do not have any Temporary Hit Points, you gain Temporary Hit Points equal to your Proficiency Bonus.\n- **Instrument.** This Instrument gains the Casting Unit and Conductive (1) Properties. Additionally, once per Long Rest, when you play this Instrument for at least a minute, you regain expended Arts Points equal to half of your maximum Arts Points.\n- **Piercing/Slashing Weapon.** You may use your Spellcasting Ability Modifier, instead of Strength or Dexterity, for the Attack and Damage Rolls with that Weapon. Additionally, you are considered Proficient with that Ritual Tool. Finally, once per Long Rest, as a Bonus Action while you are wielding this Ritual Tool, you may lose 1d4 Hit Points. If you do, you gain a bonus to your Spell Save DC and Spell Attack Rolls equal to the Hit Points you lost until the end of the Turn.\n- **Trinket.** When you fail a Saving Throw, you may reroll that Saving Throw, but must use the new result. After you succeed on a Saving Throw as a result of this reroll, you may not use this Feature again until you have completed a Short Rest.\n\n\n\n\\page\n{{pageNumber,auto}}\n\n{{wide",
